<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Yantra's mark: the bhupura (gated square) with the bindu at its centre.

  The path is the SAME geometry the app draws at runtime (bhupuraPath in YantraGlyph.kt, a 28-unit
  design space) so the launcher icon, the task glyph, the focus glyph and the notification icon are
  demonstrably one shape rather than four drawings of an idea. Here the 28 units are mapped onto the
  adaptive-icon 108dp viewport, inset to the 66dp safe zone: scale 66/28 = 2.357, offset 21dp.

  **The application icon, and the only variant with a neutral bindu.**

  Every other copy of the mark colours its centre, because the bindu is where the user's own effort
  lands and the accent is what effort looks like. This one cannot, and the reason is structural: the
  five accent icons are reached through activity-aliases, which are *components*, and a component's
  enabled state is the one thing about an icon an app may change while installed. The launcher asks
  the enabled LAUNCHER component for its icon and gets the right accent. Everything that asks the
  *package* — the notification shade, Settings, the share sheet, recents — gets this file, and
  `<application android:icon>` is fixed when the APK is built. There is no runtime, shutdown, or
  reinstall path that repoints it.

  So a coral bindu here was not the brand asserting itself; it was the mark stating an accent the
  user had not chosen, and stating it beside a notification whose own progress bar was drawn in the
  accent they had. One notification, two answers. Grey states nothing instead, which is the only
  thing this file can say that is true for all five.

  The old mark was a gold compass-rose with a tick in it; it belonged to a palette this app no
  longer has, and a tick is exactly the gesture the bindu replaced.
-->
<vector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:width="108dp"
    android:height="108dp"
    android:viewportWidth="108"
    android:viewportHeight="108">

    <group android:translateX="21" android:translateY="21"
        android:scaleX="2.357" android:scaleY="2.357">

        <!-- the gated square -->
        <path
            android:fillColor="#00000000"
            android:strokeColor="#FFB4B2A9"
            android:strokeWidth="1.5"
            android:strokeLineCap="round"
            android:strokeLineJoin="round"
            android:pathData="M8,4 L11,4 L11,2 L17,2 L17,4 L20,4 Q24,4 24,8 L24,11 L26,11 L26,17 L24,17 L24,20 Q24,24 20,24 L17,24 L17,26 L11,26 L11,24 L8,24 Q4,24 4,20 L4,17 L2,17 L2,11 L4,11 L4,8 Q4,4 8,4 Z" />

        <!-- the bindu, in the frame's own ink: see the note above on why this one cannot be an accent -->
        <path
            android:fillColor="#FFB4B2A9"
            android:pathData="M14,10.4 A3.6,3.6 0 1 1 14,17.6 A3.6,3.6 0 1 1 14,10.4 Z" />
    </group>
</vector>
